A||||13||fiero56||It Lives?!?||||5||
Z||000000||fiero56||12-14-1999||08:24 AM||||Well, I fired up the Fiero this morning(I ran it for a second last night).  When I start it up it runs at 4,000 rpm. What gives.  I have double checked every vaccume line that I know of, everything looks connected. Why is it running so fast? Throttle cable is connected properly, I believe I connected all grounds. Whats going on?||||reg||5
Z||000001||William Federle||12-14-1999||08:34 AM||||Did you have the battery disconnected since<BR>the last time you drove it? If so then you'll<BR>have to drive it 35 MPH for a little while<BR>to allow the ECM to calibrate the closed <BR>position of the idle air control valve.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||12-14-1999||09:25 AM||||Yeah, I'd say it was out for three weeks. <BR>Is the ECM thing true? I hate to start my car up and let it run cold at 4000 rpm when it is near freezing out. Plus I don't think it would be very good for my clutch with no miles on it. Anyone else want to go with what william said, or are there more theories out there.  <P>It's not that I don't believe you William(although I've never heard that either), I just want some more opinions, plus this is the first time the engine has ever been up to 4000 since I bought the car almost a year ago.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 12-14-1999).]||||reg||14
Z||000003||William Federle||12-14-1999||10:14 AM||||I had my battery disconnected for several days while I was getting my 1984 SE running<BR>and I tried everything I could think of to make it idle less than 3000 RPM until I read<BR>a posting on this forum concerning the learning that the ECM had to do in that situation. As far as the RPM goes, once you're moving the engine won't go that fast<BR>because it will be under load. Also, something I've found (and I hope it's not bad<BR>advice - maybe the ogre will read this and straighten it out if it is) is that if you<BR>put it into field svc mode (short pins A and B) after starting, the idle drops significantly. My car goes from 3000 to about<BR>1800 when I do that. You only have to do it <BR>till you get going - maybe you can rig a switch so that you can easily un-jumper it <BR>after you're moving.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Carrolles||12-14-1999||04:46 PM||||If this is a v-6 have you checked to see that your EGR is not leaking?  It sure sounds like you have a massive air leak somewhere.  You might try blanking off your EGR with a piece of sheet metal (beer can, etc).  Have you checked the EGR tube for leaks?||||reg||1
Z||000005||88formula||12-14-1999||04:53 PM||||What William says is true. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||fiero56||12-14-1999||05:37 PM||||It is a v-6('88 Formula)<BR>For those who haven't been following too clesely, my Fiero has been out of service for the last three weeks getting a new clutch.  I put it all back together yesterday, and started it up. Once the engine hit 4000 I shut it down. Figured I missed a vaccume line. <BR>I just spent the $100 on a new EGR tube, so that had better not be leaking, my old one appears to have been broken for a LONG time. <P>How long will I have to drive the car before the idle comes back down to normal(3,100 rpms lower). Isn't this bad for the engine to run so fast so cold? I don't even run it up this high when it's hot.<BR>I've never heard of the ECM needing to reprogram for the IAC.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 12-14-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||terryk||12-14-1999||07:23 PM||||Did you do the IAC reset procedure?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||William Federle||12-14-1999||07:23 PM||||I had to drive my 4 cyl 1984 Fiero about<BR>5 or 10 miles. I noticed at stop signs and<BR>stop lites that the idle was still high for about that amount of time, and then it went down to a more reasonable number.<P>I guess I don't know how long it takes for a<BR>6 cyl. car.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||fiero56||12-14-1999||10:22 PM||||Ed Parks just told me that the initial startup high idle should be no more than 1,700rpm and will drop within seconds. Mine is 4000 and doesn't drop, steadily increased to 4,000.<BR>IAC reset? Never read anything about doing it. I assume I do it through the ALDL connector? Haynes never mentioned it.||||reg||14
Z||000010||fiero56||12-15-1999||12:13 AM||||I guess I'll pull the plenum again, buy a vaccume pump, and check all the hoses. They have to be good, I didn't touch the lines. Oh well, Sears can probably hear my wallet on the way already.||||reg||9
Z||000011||william_thorniii||12-15-1999||06:27 PM||||Fiero56,<P>This sounds stupid, but have you checked the throttle cable.  I know that every once in a while, mine gets partially stuck.  Especially after it has been sitting.  Let me know if you want me to stop by and look at it.<P>William T. Thorn, III<BR>937-259-5295||||reg||5
Z||000012||fiero56||12-15-1999||06:34 PM||||I've checked the throttle cable several times.  No tension on it. I did manage to lose the little clip that holds it on though. My wrist bumped it, the clip popped off, and it never hit the ground. Amazing the things I can loose in the engine compartment. If I don't get this figured out by the week-end, maybe I'll give you a call William. Thanks for the offer. I'll remember it.||||reg||1
Z||000013||fiero56||12-15-1999||09:52 PM||||It idles beautifully now.  I pulled the plenum, and one of the gaskets was bad.  Was missing about 1/4 inch of material. After working on the car for nearly 14hrs I wasn't looking too closely at what the gasket condition was.  I put my old ones back in for now, until I get a good set. I'll try and drive it tommorrow. But, I does run good.<P>How many 20 year olds do you know who are willing to put this much time, money and effort into their cars? <P>Now to drive downtown and get a graphoil seal, Jack Walker is out of them, but he told me who does.<P>Thanks for all help, fel-pro is going to be getting a warranty item in the morning.||||reg||1
